      "intro": "If your loved one is 65 or older and lives in a household where everyone is 65 or older, they may qualify for monthly food assistance through Alabama's simplified elderly application process. Because AESAP streamlines the regular SNAP application, your loved one gets the same monthly benefits (up to $306 a month for a single person from October 1, 2026) but with a shorter 2-page form, no interview required, and benefits certified for 3 years instead of 1.\n\nThe key requirement: no one in the household can have earned income (wages or self-employment) in the month they apply. Social Security, pensions, and other unearned income are allowed. Income limits are the same as regular SNAP. From October 1, 2026, a single person qualifies with income before deductions under $1,729 a month, and there is no savings limit. Above that, your loved one can still qualify if income after deductions is under $1,330 a month and savings are under $4,750. Medical costs over $35 a month and housing costs are deducted.\n\nBecause this is still the same SNAP program underneath, your loved one gets an EBT debit card that works at grocery stores, farmers markets, and online retailers like Amazon and Walmart for eligible food purchases.",
